# Build recipe

Instructions for LLM agents building pages or components in this
codebase. Follow exactly. Human-facing rationale lives in the
*Principles*, *Foundations* and *Patterns* pages; this section is the
executable summary.

## Composing a page

1. Pick components with the [decision table](decision_table). Do not
   write raw component markup when a partial exists.
2. Render components through their `render_<component>` helper with
   explicit locals: `render_card(title: "Patient")` — block for content
   where the component yields. Each component's accepted locals are
   declared in its partial's `locals: (...)` signature on the first
   line; read it before use.
3. Lay out with container utilities: cards in `grid gap-4`, page
   regions `p-6`, never margins on components.
4. Use colour only via tokens (`bg-warning`, `text-critical`); pick
   by meaning per the *Colour* page.
5. Follow the writing rules on the *Content* page for every label,
   error and empty state.
6. Validate server-side; report action outcomes via flash (the
   layout's toast renders it); state via `alert`.

## Adding a component

New components follow the [component helpers](component_helpers)
pattern: a `render_<name>` helper as the entry point, a pure-markup
partial, a template-backed preview, and a helper unit spec. The steps
below cover the partial, preview and doc-page mechanics shared by both
the helper pattern and the older plain partials.

Only when no existing partial covers the need, and the consuming
page exists:

1. Write the request spec first:
   `spec/components/<name>_spec.rb`, `type: :request`, rendering
   `/lookbook/preview/<name>/<scenario>` and asserting DOM via
   `Capybara.string(response.body)` + `have_css`.
2. Create `app/views/gesso/components/_<name>.html.erb`: declare a strict
   `locals: (...)` signature on the first line — required
   locals without a default, optional ones with — then a `<%%# %>`
   doc comment describing each; accept `classes`; yield a block for
   content.
3. Use native elements and basecoat classes. Only if basecoat lacks
   styling: add `app/assets/tailwind/components/<name>.css` and
   register it in `app/assets/tailwind/application.css`.
4. Create `spec/components/previews/<name>_preview.rb`: `@label`, a
   `playground` with `@param` annotations, one scenario per variant.
5. Add the guidance page `spec/components/docs/05_components/
   <name>.md.erb` with the five standard sections and an embed, plus
   its `spec/docs/` spec, and a row in the decision table.
6. Run `bundle exec rspec spec/components/<name>_spec.rb spec/docs/`,
   then `bin/rails lint`. Both must pass.

## File locations

| What | Where |
| ---- | ----- |
| Component markup | `app/views/gesso/components/_<name>.html.erb` |
| Component CSS (only if needed) | `app/assets/tailwind/components/<name>.css` |
| Colour tokens | `app/assets/tailwind/theme.css` |
| Stimulus controllers | `app/javascript/controllers/` |
| Basecoat JS | bundled from `basecoat-css/all` via `app/javascript/basecoat.js` |
| Icons (lucide svg) | `app/assets/images/icons/<lucide-name>.svg` |
| Previews | `spec/components/previews/<name>_preview.rb` |
| Guidance pages | `spec/components/docs/` |
| Component specs | `spec/components/<name>_spec.rb` |
| Docs-page specs | `spec/docs/` |
